1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,933
Can you trust an AI-voiced announcement once it has been shared as a voice
note?

2
00:00:05,933 --> 00:00:11,317
RelayProof is a prototype for a careful answer, not a deepfake detector.

3
00:00:11,317 --> 00:00:20,139
Today it can play a selected file and calculate its local SHA-256, but the
receipt and matched scenario are demo data.

4
00:00:20,139 --> 00:00:29,933
It does not yet detect AudioSeal or SynthID, verify C2PA signatures, resolve
a watermark, compare a re-encoded copy, or call Monad.

5
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:32,416
The product idea has three roles.

6
00:00:32,416 --> 00:00:35,878
A publisher signs a claim about an audio file.

7
00:00:35,878 --> 00:00:42,800
A provenance mark or embedded credential can help a recipient find that
claim after sharing.

8
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:50,657
Then the recipient checks the signature, content binding, issuer status, and
any transformation evidence.

9
00:00:50,657 --> 00:01:00,533
Keep each check separate: a watermark can point to a record; it cannot prove
who made the audio or whether the announcement is true.

10
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:04,566
Existing products already cover parts of this journey.

11
00:01:04,566 --> 00:01:14,200
AIWatermark advertises audio certificates and OGG support, with
provider-reported tests that do not establish speech performance.

12
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:18,757
ContentLens claims browser-based audio soft-binding recovery.

13
00:01:18,757 --> 00:01:26,600
Digimarc’s extension documents C2PA checks for MP3 and WAV, plus selected
watermark-to-manifest recovery.

14
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:34,085
RedoSan documents several WAV watermark methods and DID tools, but its C2PA
commands are for images.

15
00:01:34,085 --> 00:01:40,222
Other projects provide local detectors, C2PA verification, or research
benchmarks.

16
00:01:40,222 --> 00:01:46,733
So the broad combination of watermark, credentials, registry, or blockchain
is not new.

17
00:01:46,733 --> 00:01:57,893
The possible test is narrower: take a clearly labeled public-information
voice clip, send it through the exact OGG or Opus transformation used by its

18
00:01:57,893 --> 00:02:02,611
sharing app, and measure recovery, false matches, and failures.

19
00:02:02,611 --> 00:02:09,801
A useful result must distinguish matched evidence, no credential found,
uncertain, and mismatch.

20
00:02:09,801 --> 00:02:16,467
No credential does not mean fake; a valid signature does not prove the claim
is truthful.

21
00:02:16,467 --> 00:02:27,303
Next: separate demo fixtures from real checks, test one end-to-end Opus
path, and ask a newsroom or publisher whether the issuer-status workflow

22
00:02:27,303 --> 00:02:28,733
solves a real need.

23
00:02:28,733 --> 00:02:31,743
Only then decide if Monad adds anything.

24
00:02:31,743 --> 00:02:36,333
Today RelayProof is still a UI prototype—not a live verifier.
